```
$ jci-coverage report --help
Writes a coverage report and prints a terminal summary. `--format`
chooses the report: `lcov` (the default) writes `coverage/lcov.info`,
`cobertura` writes `coverage/cobertura.xml`, and `both` writes both
from one test run. Requires `cargo-llvm-cov` and the `llvm-tools`
rustup component on PATH (and `cargo-nextest` too, when `--runner
nextest` is selected).

Usage: jci-coverage report [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --package <NAME>
          Limit coverage to one workspace package

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase logging verbosity

  -q, --quiet...
          Decrease logging verbosity

      --runner <RUNNER>
          Test runner to drive coverage with

          Possible values:
          - test:    `cargo llvm-cov` — plain `cargo test`
          - nextest: `cargo llvm-cov nextest` — needs `cargo-nextest`
            too
          
          [default: test]

      --nextest-profile <NAME>
          Nextest profile to use.
          
          Only meaningful with `--runner nextest`.

      --format <FORMAT>
          Report format(s) to write.
          
          `lcov` (the default) writes only `coverage/lcov.info`;
          `cobertura` writes only `coverage/cobertura.xml`; `both`
          runs the tests once and writes both files from the same
          profile data.

          Possible values:
          - lcov:      `coverage/lcov.info` only
          - cobertura: `coverage/cobertura.xml` only
          - both:      Both files, from one test run
          
          [default: lcov]

      --no-all-features
          Disable `--all-features` (on by default).
          
          Leaving it on means instrumented subprocess coverage is
          captured.

      --dry-run
          Preview the cargo-llvm-cov invocation without running it.
          
          Works with zero tool setup: never fails on a missing tool or
          touches the filesystem. Still warns (without failing) when a
          required tool is missing.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
